Subject: [PATCH 08/11] fix(session,checkout): mid-session access-revocation
 blind spot and concurrent-checkout UX

Bugsweep-01 continuation, targeted per mandatory items 2 and 7.

1. Mid-session membership/company revocation left the app blind
   (item 2: expired session / refresh token failure -- specifically
   'membership/company access removed during an active session')

   Repro: buyer logs in, is approved, uses the app normally. Staff freezes
   the company (or de-approves the buyer) in Central. The Supabase JWT
   itself is untouched -- access-token refresh keeps succeeding silently
   in the background, and BuyerSessionContext's onAuthStateChange handler
   explicitly returns early on TOKEN_REFRESHED without re-checking
   eligibility. No other code anywhere in the app calls refresh()
   periodically or on focus/foreground (confirmed: zero usages of
   useFocusEffect, AppState, or setInterval existed before this fix). The
   result: the buyer keeps seeing stale 'approved_buyer' UI (Dashboard,
   pricing, Account) indefinitely, for the rest of that app session, with
   no mechanism to ever notice their access changed short of an explicit
   logout/login or app kill+relaunch. Server-side RPC authority still
   correctly rejects any actual mutation attempt on every call (nothing
   illegitimate can succeed), but the buyer experience is confusing,
   unexplained per-action failures against UI that still claims they're an
   approved buyer.

   Fix: BuyerSessionContext now also listens to AppState and calls
   refresh() on transition to 'active' (foreground). Standard, low-risk
   pattern; also directly covers 'app reopened after a long inactive
   period' and 'background/foreground' from the same mandatory item.

2. Concurrent-checkout loser saw a misleading empty-cart message
   (item 7: confirm the DRAFT_NOT_FOUND loser gets an accurate,
   actionable message, not a misleading one)

   The core race was already verified defect-free (first-updater-wins via
   FOR UPDATE + status transition in submit_customer_order_v1 -- see the
   prior bugsweep report). But the losing submitter's client showed the
   generic 'No active order draft was found. Add items from the
   catalogue.' -- accurate for a genuinely empty cart, misleading here: it
   implies nothing was ever in the cart, when in fact an order very likely
   WAS just created (by another device on the same account, or this same
   device on a dropped-response retry).

   Fix: CheckoutScreen now recognizes the DRAFT_NOT_FOUND code specifically
   in the checkout-submit path (not the general add-to-cart path, where the
   original message remains correct) and shows 'This cart may have just
   been submitted -- by you on another device, or a teammate on your
   account. Check Orders for the new Sales Order before adding these items
   again,' with a 'View Orders' action button. There is no reliable way to
   deterministically look up which order resulted (the other submitter
   used a different idempotency key), so this is the honest, actionable
   recovery rather than a guess.

No test added for either: both require either supabase/react-native
runtime (BuyerSessionContext, same established constraint as prior fixes)
or React Navigation rendering (the CheckoutScreen JSX branch) that this
repo's plain node:test convention doesn't cover.

Verified: npm run quality passes end to end -- 164/164 tests (unchanged),
typecheck clean, lint clean, boundary/rc-scan/rc-navigation/cert:bindings/
census:p106/expo-config all pass. Diff inspected: only the two intended
files changed, no service-role usage, no secrets, no RLS touched, no
unrelated changes.

No production mutation of any kind.
